A||N||7||V6pack||Lowering springs...How difficult?||||5||
Z||000000||V6pack||03-01-2000||12:50 PM||||Hi all.  I'm looking to lower the front of one of my Fieros.  Can't seem to find the drop spindles anywhere.  I am going to install spings I guess.  How hard is it to do?  I did a search and looked around the lists, and found a lot about brands etc... did not find much about what is involved or if a shade tree guy should even attempt it.<P>Thanks,<BR>v6pack||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-01-2000||05:30 PM||||just the front???    anywho..  after properly supporting the car on jack stands and taking the wheels off, take the nut off the stering arm (cotter pinned in place)(forward most arm) then remove the nut from the ball joint (lower most nut) on the inside of your front hub. support the lower a-arm then remove the upper bolts from the shock... SLOWLY release the pressure from the lower a-arm and then the spring will with a bit of tugging come out of its seat and bring the upper isolation ring with it... remove this ring reapply it to your new spring and reverse the order to re-assemble. It took I and my girlfriend about 35 min per side on BlackGTs car just last weekend. We had done it on my car about 18 months prior... This will toss off your toe in and camber so you  will need an alignment... It may "feel" ok but i assure you it's not! Your nice new tires will be TOAST in 500 miles! ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||03-02-2000||12:17 AM||||Unless the sway bar end links look in perfect shape, this is a good time to replace those with poly ones too. Disconnect the sway bar and lower front shock bolt from the lower control arms.<P>Unless the tie rod ends need replaced, the front coil springs can be changed without removing them. Separate the lower ball joint from the spindle. Now is a good time to replace the lower ball joints which usually wear more rapidly than the upper ones.<P>Let the lower control arm swing all the way down. Pull and pry on the coil spring like 1fst2m6 said until the spring pops out. The new ones are easier to put back in because they're shorter. Make sure the ends of the spring sits down in the pocket like it's supposed to. An afternoon job at least.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-02-2000||01:26 AM||||I just changed mine to the lower springs. To remove the original ones buy a good floor jack and get the lower A-arm 16" above the ground. This way the A-arm will swing all the way down and the springs come out very easy. Also I suggest you get a bike chain to wrap around spring and frame just in case spring "lets loose". It can shoot out at a very high rate of speed. Just a safety tip.||||reg||1
Z||000004||V6pack||03-02-2000||04:40 AM||||Thanks guys.  The Haynes manual doesn't say anything about springs....I guess they last forever huh?  Anyway, I'll be careful with the springs and replace those items you mentioned while I'm in there.  <P>The nose of this GT now sits at 10 1/2" off the garage floor.  Looks like it is about to take off :-)  4" higher than my other one.  Don't know whats up with that, but I guess I'll find out when I get under there.  I appreciate the tips!<P>v6pack||||reg||12
Z||000005||Monkeyman||03-02-2000||06:59 AM||||If the bottom of your car is 10 1/2" off the ground, there is definitely something wrong with your car!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||03-02-2000||10:17 AM||||If the car is 16" off the ground the lower A-arm will swing staight down to remove the spring. If your less than that the A-arm will not swing all the way down and you'll need to pry the old spring off the stop. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||V6pack||03-02-2000||06:15 PM||||Yeah Monk...I think you are right.  It seems to be way too high.  Maybe the wrong shocks put on by the PO?  I'm okay with the easy stuff, but suspension is a mystery to me.  Everything appears straight.  I guess I will find out when I get into it.<P>Thanks<BR>v6pack||||reg||10
